## Free NHS dental treatment

You do not pay for NHS dental treatment if, when treatment starts, you:

- are **under 18**
- are **18 and in full-time education**
- are **pregnant** or have had a **baby in the last 12 months**
- are being treated in an **NHS hospital** by a hospital dentist (you may pay for dentures or bridges)
- get **income-related ESA**, **Pension Credit Guarantee Credit**, or **Universal Credit** within the earnings limits
- have an **HC2** certificate (an **HC3** may help with part of the cost)

## NHS dental charges in England

If you pay, NHS charges are set in **three bands**, depending on the treatment you need:

- **Band 1:** check-ups, X-rays, scale and polish, and urgent care
- **Band 2:** everything in Band 1, plus fillings, root canal treatment and taking out teeth
- **Band 3:** everything in Bands 1 and 2, plus crowns, dentures and bridges

You pay **one charge for each course of treatment**, however many visits it takes. Check the current prices on the NHS website.

## In Wales

**Dental check-ups are free** if you are **under 25** or **60 or over**. Wales changed how dental charges work in **April 2026**, with set charges for different types of care and a maximum charge for a course of treatment. Ask your dentist what you will pay before treatment starts.

## Finding an NHS dentist

Search for dentists taking on NHS patients on the NHS website, or call **NHS 111** if you need **urgent dental care** and cannot find a dentist. In Wales, contact your local health board.

